WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.153
Hi, I'm Kumquat!

00:00:02.153 --> 00:00:06.180
A decision model like Jev never writes: it picks from a list you send.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.330
Here's where it fits.

00:00:10.475 --> 00:00:14.257
The key fact: each option is scored alone, and options meet only

00:00:14.257 --> 00:00:15.517
in a final softmax.

00:00:15.517 --> 00:00:17.408
So no option can read another.

00:00:21.342 --> 00:00:22.933
Your code builds the option list.

00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:24.790
The model can only answer from it.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.608
Each option is encoded in its own row, beside the state and

00:00:28.608 --> 00:00:30.590
question, and comes out as one number.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:35.155
The options first meet in a softmax, after every forward pass has finished.

00:00:35.575 --> 00:00:39.787
Then code does the rest: take the top option, or branch on its probability.

00:00:41.342 --> 00:00:45.471
The vendor drew the same procedure: regex finds candidate values, and downstream

00:00:45.471 --> 00:00:47.535
code acts on the one picked.

00:00:47.955 --> 00:00:50.860
The highlighted box is the only part the model does: pick

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:51.917
the one that fits.

00:00:53.858 --> 00:00:56.773
One: is the answer set finite, and known before the call?

00:00:57.193 --> 00:00:59.021
Two: can code you own enumerate it?

00:00:59.021 --> 00:01:00.327
That's where the work is.

00:01:00.747 --> 00:01:03.483
Three: can each option be judged without reading another?

00:01:03.483 --> 00:01:06.827
If not, relate them in code, and ask about one pair.

00:01:07.247 --> 00:01:09.788
Four: is the list frozen before you branch?

00:01:09.788 --> 00:01:13.600
Add paraphrases of the right answer, and its probability falls from point

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.142
six one nine to point two one five.

00:01:17.758 --> 00:01:21.994
Where one option refers to another, Jev scores zero of a hundred.

00:01:21.994 --> 00:01:26.229
Letting options see each other isn't enough: a bidirectional encoder scores eight.

00:01:27.808 --> 00:01:30.548
The test is whether one option can read another.

00:01:30.548 --> 00:01:33.896
Cost and latency aren't gates; money can't buy this one back.

00:01:35.858 --> 00:01:39.825
To recap: code builds the list, each option is scored alone, and

00:01:39.825 --> 00:01:41.148
relations belong in code.

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:43.713
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:43.713 --> 00:01:44.325
I'm Kumquat.

00:01:44.325 --> 00:01:44.632
Bye!
